hello my name is Rahul Mandalay and I'll  leave new business creation within  deloitte's life sciences and healthcare  practice with me is my colleague Jurgen  Planck who is our precision medicine  leader we are here today to examine the  future of biomedical research we are  going to look both at how biomedical  research has been conducted to date and  how the landscape is changing as well as  a potential solution that we are using  to address this innovative field  biomedical research is at a unique  juncture it is increasingly turning to  data science to make new discoveries for  treatments let me give you three  examples of drivers that are causing  this change and the resulting needs for  data science first there are large  volumes of data now available orders of  magnitude more than before that need to  be stored for efficient use this  includes the traditional sets of science  generated data such as genomics data  imaging data clinical data but also  patient generated data from variables  and environmental data and explore  exposure data these data are now being  collected in ever increasing quantities  and there is an exception expectation  that if they can be easily stored and  analyzed they can help drive new  discoveries and treatment options thus  there is a huge need for storage and  efficient search capabilities for  biomedical data second there is an  interest in driving deeper into  precision medicine by using artificial  intelligence researchers want to  understand how the body functions at all  levels starting at the molecular level  then at the cellular level and the organ  level take Cystic Fibrosis is an example  researchers found the correlation  between the CFTR gene and the disease  leading to the understanding that a  mutation in the CFTR gene was the cause  of cystic fibrosis from this discovery a  new treatment could be developed and two  years ago the FDA granted a breakthrough  therapy designation  to a particular gene therapy that  addressed the mutated gene however in  most cases the relation between genetic  mutation and disease is much more  complex it is not a one mutated gene  causes one disease situation so  researchers need to go further they need  to understand how the body functions at  the molecular level looking at all genes  and proteins in action  within cells and organs this will  require the development of new  artificial intelligence methods that can  analyze all of the data available  thus biomedical research is becoming a  huge computational task third there are  an estimated 7,000 rare diseases of  which only 500 have treatments and even  fewer of cures thus there's an urgent  call to researchers to share all of  their data to rapidly advance the  discovery of new treatments while each  of these 7,000 diseases may be rare  individually the total tally affects a  significant number across the population  to not be learned that cancer is not  just one disease but rather it is a  collection of many different types of  cancer some of which are very rare we  need to understand what is happening to  the body with each of these different  cancer types in order to find cures by  sharing the data with the research  community there is an opportunity to  perform new analysis and make new  discoveries let me summarize - make new  discoveries and find new treatments  biomedical researchers will need to be  able to store analyze and share all of  their data at scale we are now going to  look at a particular use case that  focuses on the necessary steps to share  data without compromising privacy the  biomedical data be used for research are  collected from humans and ensuring the  privacy of these data dollars is  critical without it we would put at risk  the trust given to scientists by these  data donors  the HIPAA Privacy Rule establishes the  conditions under which protected health  information or pH I may be used or  disclosed for research purposes in short  HIPAA requires that all pahi be  de-identified before data are shared in  addition other laws require that all  personally identifiable information or  PII also be be identified our use case  focuses on the D identification of  imaging data for cancer cardiovascular  and pulmonary research a critical step  before these images can be shared with  the research community on this slide you  will see a pair of lung x-rays today  most CT scans MRIs and x-rays use the  DICOM standard which stands for digital  imaging and communications and medicine  DICOM data has a header which includes  pH I and PII data see the red boxes on  the top left and right in addition the  pH I and PII can be found on the image  itself see the red box at the bottom we  are working with our client on a pilot  to ensure their patient's data is  de-identified by removing masking or  encrypting the sensitive PhIP III data  before images are shared to do this we  are using Google's healthcare API as an  out-of-the-box solution in our pilot we  are working to answer two questions how  reliable is the D identification process  and what is achievable throughput of the  process this is important as we are  working to do this at scale as you can  see de-identified data just from images  is a large undertaking and this is only  one of many steps that must be taken to  prepare data for large-scale analytic  efforts if we take a step back and look  at all the steps required for biomedical  research we realize that what we need is  an efficient solution that provides the  biomedical community  but the ability to store analyze and  share their data at scale we will now  discuss how we can build such an  environment that meets these needs on  the Google cloud platform  however we should note that a technical  solution alone will not be sufficient to  create a tithing ecosystem of biomedical  research that widely shares their data  there are several non technical  challenges that must be overcome but  they are beyond the scope of this  presentation I'm now going to hand over  to virgon to walk us through the details  of the GCP based solution thanks for who  to start let's examine the principles of  a biomedical research data science  solution as Raul mentioned there are  three key functional requirements the  ability to effectively store and manage  data the ability to analyze data at  scale and the ability to share data with  the community and effectively used to  share data let's look at each of these  three in turn starting with data  organization a foundational principle  for organizing biomedical data is the  concept of fair data fair stands for  findable accessible interoperable and  reusable let's look at each one each of  these mean fo findable a for accessible  eye for interoperable and R for reusable  starting with findable this means that  all data must be annotated with metadata  that makes the data searchable this must  be possible for all data types the data  should be stored in a data lake and we  need a proper metadata repository data  that is newly ingested should ideally be  annotated with metadata in an automated  or semi-automated way to allow data  ingest at scale finally we need an  efficient search  mechanism that lets us discover data of  interest this must be possible for all  data today most data sit in silos that  cannot be searched from the outside the  next one is a for accessible this means  that there must be an easy way to  request access to data that has been  discovered and is of interest ideally we  don't want to move the data around so  data access really means that I can use  the data to compute on it and quite  possibly compute across many data sets  that may reside in different locations  what we have today again is still mostly  data silos where it is impossible to run  algorithms across the data sets the  third fare data concept is AI for  interoperable now this is where data  standards come into play  unfortunately in biomedical research  there's a myriad of standards at a  minimum data should adhere to a common  standard and we need mappings between  the most common standards and yes by  data standards I also mean semantic  standards such as anthologies and  vocabularies not just syntactic  standards I is probably the hardest of  the four fair principles finally are for  reusable this has to do with data use  agreements and consents and accompany  the data it must be possible to trace  what data are used for so that any  misuse can be discovered in appropriate  measures to protect the data and the  human data donors can be taken when it  comes to the second requirement powerful  data analysis we have to realize that  algorithms are actually just data as  well and thus their codes should also  follow fair principles because in the  end we not only want to share the raw  data but also our analysis  since in order to accelerate innovation  we need an easy-to-use data science  environment that allows us to scale ibly  compute across all of the data of  interest furthermore we want to be able  to reuse already existing algorithms  many of these are encoded in the form of  services that are natively available on  the Google cloud platform the previously  discussed the identification service is  such an algorithm and we need to be able  to access it so the rapidly through its  API in an easy way many other algorithms  are bundled into libraries this could be  machine learning and artificial  intelligence methods for image analysis  or for a genomic study again these  algorithms or libraries must be easily  accessible from within the data science  environment the last requirement is data  sharing we already discussed this as  part of the fair principles but to  reiterate sharing must be possible not  only within teams but also across teams  and even between institutions if we  follow these guiding principles when  building a solution on the Google cloud  platform we will have enabled the  biomedical research community to focus  on what they are best at doing their  research without having to worry about  the underlying technology and hopefully  be able to accelerate discoveries and  new treatment options for the many  untreatable diseases that ALS we have  developed a DCP native reference  implementation for biomedical research  solution that follows the principles we  just discussed you can see some  screenshots on the right and that will  show an architectural diagram later on  the data types we have to handle include  clinical data in the form of electronic  health records often now following the  fire standard genomic data medical and  scientific imaging data radio  images follow the DICOM standard but  pathology images do not consistently  follow that standard and we have also  handled medical supply chain and public  health data labs mostly as part of our  work for the Kobe 19 healthcare  coalition which is a pro bono effort of  almost 1,000 organizations coming  together to share data and perform  studies to combat Kobe 19 we're also  supporting a variety of analytic methods  researchers often prefer to use a  notebook like environment to code their  methods and workflows specifically  Jupiter notebooks that can decode it in  R or in Python to select datasets and  build cohorts but also to generate  downstream analyses bigquery can be  easily used  we also discussed access to  microservices via their API it's a  Google cloud platform  through its many healthcare and  biomedical products offers access to  many services ranging from genomic  analyses to data mapping to image  analytics to advanced machine learning  and artificial intelligence and more  these are powerful tools that we can  bring to the researchers fingertips and  finally we offer the ability to reuse  existing workflows for sharing enabling  true collaboration in method and  algorithm development  now let's take a closer look at the  solution you can see here two  screenshots the Left screenshot shows  the home screen where you can see all of  your projects listed projects are the  big tiles on the screen which show the  project name a short description and the  approved users on the right screenshot  we open one project at the top you can  see the project information again on the  left is the metadata repository which is  searchable and where it can select data  for use as well as share data with  others and on the right side of the  workspace is where I can launch Jupiter  notebooks to perform your analyses  what's important is that this user  interface is intuitive and takes away  from the complexity of the Google cloud  platform itself if I showed the GCP home  screen to a biomedical researcher they  would be overwhelmed by the choices of  tools and often not understand how to  use them how our user interface provides  them with an intuitive screen that they  can understand how to navigate and  perform their work  all the while using the full power of  TCP behind it let's take a look at the  architecture I'll start on the left side  this is where we receive data from  systems such as microscopes or other  devices that can stream their data right  into our environment we can use a  command-line interface to push the data  with a defined frequency or we can  manually load the data the ingest  process uses cloud network and cloud  data fusion to push the data into a  Google cloud storage bucket the metadata  repository near the middle of the slide  serves as an environment to store all  metadata associated with the collected  data upon data ingest the data can be  inspected for tags for example  fire or die contacts that can be used to  automatically generate metadata we use  services provided by the Google  healthcare a P I to the left of the  metadata repository to perform initial  transformations of the data such as the  identification  this requires temporary placement of the  data into a fire or die chrome store  where the D identification service is  being performed we have a data sharing  capability that invokes pops-up firebase  and can leverage services such as  Google's data marketplace to make data  discoverable for search near the top of  the slide we use Apache Solr  and to the right we have notebook  services that utilize GCP stupid lab to  launch notebook services in these  notebooks all the way on the right in  the second box from the top the  researchers can code and execute their  algorithms they can also access GCP  services through what we call  convenience methods which are wrappers  around the api's so that researchers  don't have to learn the detailed command  syntax of an API call but instead can  use a very simple and intuitive commands  have just de-identified with some simple  arguments for security we use the native  GCP security layer which provides  Identity and Access Management  and offers a very granular role based  data access cost management is also  built-in to allow researchers to see the  cost of a service before they use it  above all we strive to follow the open  community's best practices data will not  end up in a proprietary silo rather it  sits in a GCS bucket and can be accessed  and reused from any other environment  similarly compute uses native TCP  components which will work across the  stored in GCS what we hope to illustrate  with this reference implementation is to  demonstrate how we can stop building  more proprietary data silos in  biomedical research that will just  prevent the community from collaborating  efficiently if we follow the standards  and best practices we laid out solutions  like our reference implementation will  enable the construction of a  collaborative biomedical research  environment that can help researchers  accelerate discoveries and treatment now  I'll hand it back to Rahul to close out  our discussion thanks Jordan  and thanks Google for the opportunity  for this discussion today in summary  we're previewed how biomedical research  is transforming into a data science we  discussed the principles of how  biomedical data can be effectively  managed and analyzed in the Google cloud  platform and B showed you a reference  implementation in addition we discussed  how Google cloud platform allows us to  do biomedical research in a highly  scalable and cost-effective way in the  cloud and finally and most importantly a  cloud native solution for researchers to  collaborate and share their data with  one another is vitally important to  accelerate scientific discovery and find  new treatments for the many still  untreatable diseases  if you have any questions on this  presentation or the content please feel  free to reach out to us directly we have  included our contact information on this  slide thanks again for your time and  interest
 

